I deleted something on GitHub, and now my RStudio won't push to GitHub; it keeps saying rejected!

That "rejected" error almost always means your local branch and the GitHub remote have diverged — usually because whatever you deleted on GitHub changed the remote's history, so Git won't let you push over it blindly. Good news: it's very fixable.

A few things to check first:

What exactly did you delete on GitHub?

A file (deleted via the GitHub web interface)?
The whole repository, then recreated it?
A branch?
The fix differs depending on which.

Most common case: you deleted a file directly on GitHub's website

That creates a new commit on GitHub that your local repo doesn't have. Git rejects your push because your local history and the remote history don't line up. Fix:

r
# In RStudio's Terminal tab (not Console)
git pull origin main --rebase
(replace main with your branch name if different — could be master)

Then push again:

r
git push origin main
If you get merge conflicts during the rebase, RStudio will show you the conflicted files — you'd need to resolve those manually.

If you recreated the repo from scratch on GitHub (deleted it and made a new one), your local repo is pointing to a remote that essentially has unrelated history. Fix:

r
git push origin main --force
⚠️ Only do this if you're sure the GitHub version has nothing you need — force push overwrites the remote with your local version entirely.

If you deleted a branch on GitHub

Push might be complaining about a different issue — check the exact error text.
